WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:07.750
This is a building, in a town, on an island,
in a territory, in the South Pacific—an

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:13.459
ocean so vast that barely any planes transverse
it; barely any ships sail through it; an area

00:00:13.459 --> 00:00:18.439
so empty that when we talk about how many
people live where we look now, we do not talk

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:24.009
in magnitudes of billions or even millions,
we talk about the mere thousands of people

00:00:24.009 --> 00:00:27.490
who live in this immense precinct of our world.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:33.890
But in a territory, on an island, in a town,
in these buildings live just fifty of them.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:39.149
Fifty people, on an island, where the only
way in or out is on a boat that comes a few

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:40.850
times a year.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:45.360
These people live hundreds of miles away from
the closest other people, thousands of miles

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.219
away from the closest city, and ten thousand
miles or sixteen thousand kilometers away

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:52.320
from their country’s capital in London.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:58.880
That’s because this is Pitcairn Island—Britain’s
smallest, furthest, and most isolated overseas

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.129
territory.

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:04.610
These fifty people live a life so removed
from those of Belfasters or Edinburghers or

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.970
Cardiffians or Londoners that believing that
they’re part of the same country is just

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.330
as hard as believing that Pitcairn itself
even exists.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:16.520
That’s because to all rational minds Pitcairn
shouldn’t exist.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:21.330
It just doesn’t make sense for there be
an island of fifty people living in the UK

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.730
yet on the absolute extremity of earth.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:27.550
That’s why I sought to make sense of it.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:33.330
In the first season of our new podcast, Extremities,
I explore over six episodes why and how Pitcairn

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:37.930
exists—why people settled there, why it
became part of the UK, why people still live

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.280
there, how they get there today, how they
get food, how they make money, how their government

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.720
works, how they communicate, how they plan
to continue existing, and much much more.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:51.060
You can find Extremities on all major podcasting
apps, at ExtremitiesPodcast.com, or links

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.640
are in the description.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.210
This is just season one and in future seasons,
we’ll be exploring why and how other of

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.920
earth’s most isolated settlements exist.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.070
This is something I and others have been working
on for a while so I do hope you give at least

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.530
episode one a listen.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:10.099
I believe it’s something any Wendover Productions
viewer will enjoy so I can’t wait to hear

00:02:10.099 --> 00:02:10.929
what you think.

